# Keep the repo succinct

Only add or keep material that helps **other engineers** understand and mod FF7 PSX discs. When in doubt, leave it out or put it in chat, not git.

## Do not commit

- Personal machine setup (SSH keys, git identity, account-specific remotes, IDE preferences)
- Session narrative, “how we got here,” or duplicate instructions already in rules/skills
- Generic tool docs with no FF7/PSX-specific detail
- New files when a short addition to an existing doc is enough
- Redundant READMEs / WINDOWS-INSTRUCTIONS / handoff sprawl — root README + AGENTS.md are enough
- Empty folder placeholders as README.md — use `.gitkeep` under `workspace/`

## Do commit

- FF7 disc format, file layout, addresses, patch workflow, verified RE results
- Scripts and commands that are part of the modding workflow
- Findings with **project-specific** facts (offsets, behaviors, test results)
- Active **`docs/windows-last-output.txt`** when used as the git output pipe (overwrite each run)
- Published **`builder/`** JSON packs + `mods/<mod>/` source

## Writing style

- Short, scannable reference — tables and bullets over prose
- One home per fact: reference doc OR finding, not both with the same text
- README and `AGENTS.md` are indexes; deep detail lives in `docs/0N-*.md`, `docs/findings/`, or `mods/*/patches/`
- Trim or delete docs that no longer earn their keep

## Before adding anything

Ask: *Would a new contributor need this to mod the disc, or is it only useful to me on this machine?* If the latter, do not add it.
